title: 'statement-store: skip naturally-expired hashes from expired map'
doc:
- audience: Node Dev
  description: "## Problem\n\nImplement #11665\n\nThe statement store maintains an\
    \ `expired: HashMap<Hash, u64>` to suppress\nre-gossip of removed statements.\
    \ It grew unboundedly because:\n\n1. **All** removed statements were inserted\
    \ regardless of removal reason.\n   Statements removed because their own expiry\
    \ timestamp had already passed\n   were included even though `submit` rejects\
    \ them unconditionally via\n   `AlreadyExpired` \u2014 the map was never consulted\
    \ for them.\n\n2. The purge (`maintain`) used `HashMap::retain`, which is O(n)\
    \ over the\n   entire map. With `purge_after_sec = 48h` and high eviction rates\
    \ the map\n   could reach ~100M entries / ~9GB RAM, with a full scan every 29\
    \ seconds.\n\n\n## Changes\n\n`make_expired` now checks `current_time < expiry.get_expiration_timestamp_secs()`\n\
    before inserting into the expired map. Statements whose own timestamp has\nalready\
    \ passed are skipped in both the in-memory map and `col::EXPIRED` on\ndisk.\n\n\
    The three DB-write sites (`submit`, `remove`, `remove_by`) gate the\n`col::EXPIRED`\
    \ write on `index.expired.contains(&hash)`, matching the\nin-memory decision.\n\
    \nO(log n + k) purge via ExpiredIndex\n\nAdded `Expiry::get_expiration_timestamp_secs()`**"
crates:
- name: sc-statement-store
  bump: patch
